  {
    "episode_id": 73245,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/3/73245.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 15,
    "turn_text": "But the alliance has to be strong because it is our mutual defense. You rightly bring up Article 5. They all came with us into Afghanistan when we decided the country. The president decided that was the route we would go. And that's why it's so strange for this president, President Trump, to go to NATO and in front of the building there, which by the way has sculptures about 9-11 on the outside of it to go there and say that he questioned our commitment to Article 5 of the NATO alliance. That was a stunning thing. He later kind of backed off it a bit, but that was his first statement when he was there. So that was frightening. I mean, there's no other word I can use except frightening because it's scary that the United States would not be committed to Article 5 when everyone else was committed to it for us. NATO is strong, and then the Europeans are forming other kinds of interaction between countries and among countries of Europe to be strong as well. So again, that's not a minus thing to us, it's encouraging.",
