  {
    "episode_id": 73245,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/3/73245.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 25,
    "turn_text": "Well, I think there are many of us who have that reassuring role. I will say that I have worked in an inter-parliamentary way for years, so I know a large number of these people over a period of time. Of course, there are many new people on the scene in our country and in theirs. But for my earliest days in Congress was a member of the North Atlantic Assembly. inter-parliamentary group of the NATO countries. I've hosted one of the committees in San Francisco as a newest member of Congress. So I have relationships there. The McCain role was a very unique one in that the times in which he participated were, shall we say, were questioning. There were some questions, and I was so honored to be in In Munich this year, because he always went to the Munich Security Conference, and this year they gave an award in his honor, which was lovely. He was a veteran. He had fought in the wars. He was a prisoner of war. He had standing that I think it's very hard for anybody to say, I'm feeling a role, but he felt, on the other hand, I think there are many of us. who in our interactions with our fellow parliamentarians, in my case with the presidents of the parliaments, have a responsibility to show the respect we have for their countries, the recognition that we need to be multilateral in our approaches if we're going to be sustainable. And that we have one great big threat to this whole planet, and that is the climate crisis. And that's a health issue, a public health issue. The air with our children breathe, the water they drink. It's a jobs issue for us to have new green technology, lift everyone up. It's a security issue. There's droughts and floods and famine and the rest create competition for habitat and resources and cause migrations that pose security problems. And it's a moral issue. If you believe aside that this is God's creation, we have a moral responsibility to be good stewards of it. If you don't share that religious point of view, everybody agrees we have a moral responsibility future generation. So we have a difference with this president who says science has no role in our decisions on these issues. No. Science has a big role, and I say that as a devout like Catholic person. Here they say science or faith. I say science is an answer to our prayers. So we find our common ground one way or another with our colleagues and other countries. We emphasized in this last meeting once again the importance of inter-parliamentary interactions to save the world.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_02",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
